            <!-- Allocation Context -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">How BC's 2026 Allocation Was Set</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm space-y-5">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The federal Immigration Levels Plan 2025-2027, announced in October 2024, cut the national Provincial Nominee Program (PNP) target from 110,000 in 2024 to 55,000 in 2025. The 2026-2028 plan raised the 2026 target to 91,500. Within that, IRCC gave British Columbia 5,254 nominations for 2026 (announced December 18, 2025; BC had asked for 9,000), then added 1,000 on August 18, 2026, for a total of 6,254. The province decides how to use that allocation across its Skills Immigration and Entrepreneur Immigration streams.</p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">On April 23, 2026, BC PNP published its priorities around three objectives: <strong>Care</strong> (36 occupations in health care, education, childcare and veterinary care), <strong>Build</strong> (certified workers in 9 construction trades) and <strong>Innovate</strong> (High Economic Impact invitations across all sectors, including tech). It said at least 35% of nominations are expected to go outside Metro Vancouver, confirmed that the final priority tech draw took place on December 3, 2024, and said no new student streams will launch. The Entry Level and Semi-Skilled stream is closed. BC PNP does not publish stream-level quotas.</p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The draws show how the allocation was used. Innovate points cutoffs ran from 138 in February to 131 in September and October, and the wage track fell from $70/hr and $145,000/yr to $49/hr and $99,000/yr (October 29). Targeted Care and Build rounds reached much lower scores: Care: Health fell from 108 in May to 76, Childcare from 115 to 99, and Construction from 108 to a low of 88 before settling at 94. Health Authority stream applicants apply directly, without a registration or invitation.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Stream Breakdown Table -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">2026 Invitations by Draw Type</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">BC PNP does not publish stream quotas. Our January estimates by stream were replaced by what actually happened: Skills Immigration invitations published on WelcomeBC from February 4 to October 29, 2026 (our sum, at least 7,085). Invitations are not nominations, and Health Authority and Entrepreneur nominations are not included.</p>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
                        <li class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-100 pb-2"><span>Innovate: High Economic Impact, wage track</span><strong>2,518 (about 36%)</strong></li>
                        <li class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-100 pb-2"><span>Innovate: High Economic Impact, points track</span><strong>2,228 (about 31%)</strong></li>
