                        <li class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-100 pb-2"><span>Care: Health</span><strong>699 (about 10%)</strong></li>
                        <li class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-100 pb-2"><span>Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative</span><strong>109 (about 1.5%)</strong></li>
                        <li class="flex justify-between border-b border-slate-100 pb-2"><span>Care: Veterinary (plus three rounds of fewer than 5)</span><strong>21+ (under 1%)</strong></li>
                        <li class="flex justify-between pt-2"><span>Care: Education</span><strong>Fewer than 5</strong></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Priority Occupations -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Priority Occupations Targeted in 2026</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm space-y-4">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Since April 23, 2026, targeted Care and Build rounds invite the highest-scoring registrants within priority occupations, which is why their cutoffs sit well below the Innovate points cutoff. Examples (see WelcomeBC's selection of workers page for the full lists):</p>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700">
                        <li><strong>Physicians and Specialists (NOC 31102, 31100):</strong> Health Authority stream applicants apply directly, without a registration or invitation.</li>
                        <li><strong>Registered Nurses and Psychiatric Nurses (NOC 31301, 31302):</strong> Health Authority stream if employed by a health authority; otherwise Care: Health draws (cutoffs 108 to 76 in 2026).</li>
                        <li><strong>Nurse Practitioners (NOC 31302):</strong> priority within Health Authority stream.</li>
                        <li><strong>Allied Health (NOC 31201, 31203, 32109, 32101, 32104):</strong> physiotherapists, occupational therapists, medical lab technologists, paramedics.</li>
                        <li><strong>Early Childhood Educators (NOC 42202):</strong> Care: Childcare draws for certified ECEs (cutoffs 115 to 99 in 2026).</li>
                        <li><strong>Construction Tradespeople:</strong> Build draws for 9 priority trades with SkilledTradesBC certification or a registered apprenticeship (cutoffs 108 to 88 in 2026).</li>
                        <li><strong>Technology occupations:</strong> no longer a priority list. They remain eligible and compete in Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Strategic Response -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">How Applicants Should Respond</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm space-y-5">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">With lower overall allocation, applicants outside priority occupations need to plan more carefully. A few strategic moves can dramatically improve odds in 2026.</p>
                    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-slate-700">
                        <li><strong>Align with a priority occupation if possible.</strong> A registered nurse, certified tradesperson, or early childhood educator now has substantially better odds than a general TEER 2 office worker.</li>
                        <li><strong>Outside Care and Build, aim for 131+ or the wage line.</strong> Innovate points cutoffs were 131 to 138 in 2026, and the wage track fell to $49/hr and $99,000/yr on October 29.</li>
                        <li><strong>Consider regional BC.</strong> The Northeast Pilot, Kelowna, Vancouver Island, and Northern Development Initiative regions all carry SIRS bonus points and lower competition.</li>
                        <li><strong>Apply Express Entry BC where eligible.</strong> Federal Express Entry-linked PR processing remains fast (5-8 months) while non-EE PR drags 11-18 months.</li>
                        <li><strong>Get language to CLB 9.</strong> Strong language scores feed both SIRS and federal CRS, providing a hedge across both systems.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 2026 Outlook FAQ -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">2026 Allocation FAQ</h2>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 open:border-green-200 open:shadow-md transition-all">
                        <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-6 cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Will BC PNP get more allocations later in 2026?</span><span class="text-green-600">▼</span></summary>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes, it already has. On August 18, 2026, IRCC added 1,000 nominations, raising BC's 2026 allocation from 5,254 to 6,254. The BC PNP says it plans to use the full allocation by December 31, 2026.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 open:border-green-200 open:shadow-md transition-all">
                        <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-6 cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Does the 2026 allocation include the Tech stream?</span><span class="text-green-600">▼</span></summary>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">There are no tech-only draws in 2026. The BC PNP says the final priority tech draw took place on December 3, 2024. Tech occupations remain eligible under the Skilled Worker stream and count toward the 6,254 allocation, but candidates compete in Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds with every other sector.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 open:border-green-200 open:shadow-md transition-all">
                        <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-6 cursor-pointer list-none"><span>What happens to applications already in the queue?</span><span class="text-green-600">▼</span></summary>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Applications submitted before the allocation cut continue to be processed normally. The 2026 cap affects only new nominations issued in 2026. Existing nominations remain valid until their 6-month expiry.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 open:border-green-200 open:shadow-md transition-all">
                        <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-6 cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Is Entrepreneur Immigration affected by the cap?</span><span class="text-green-600">▼</span></summary>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. Entrepreneur nominations count toward BC's total allocation. The Base and Regional streams continue with small, roughly monthly rounds; the latest, on October 20, 2026, invited 10 Base candidates and fewer than 5 Regional candidates at a minimum score of 118.</div>
                    </details>
                    <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 open:border-green-200 open:shadow-md transition-all">
                        <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-6 cursor-pointer list-none"><span>Will the 2027 plan increase allocations?</span><span class="text-green-600">▼</span></summary>
                        <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The federal 2027-2029 Levels Plan, released on November 5, 2026, sets the national PNP target at 95,000 a year, up from 91,500 in 2026. It also adds a one-time 2027 measure under which provinces can nominate workers already in Canada through expanded PNP allocations. BC's own 2027 allocation had not been announced as of November 11, 2026.</div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Pro Tips Specific to 2026 -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Three Practical Moves This Quarter</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm space-y-3">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">If you are already in the SIRS pool and watching the cut-offs creep upward, three practical moves help most often. First, re-take your language test to push to CLB 9, which adds both SIRS and CRS points and signals stronger settlement readiness. Second, negotiate a wage increase above the regional median for your NOC; even a $2-3/hr bump can unlock 4-6 SIRS points and tip a borderline application into the next draw. Third, check whether moving to a smaller BC city unlocks regional bonus points worth 5-10 SIRS points, especially in Prince George, Fort St. John, or Cranbrook where employers actively support PNP relocation.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Allocation Math -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Allocation Math Explained</h2>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm space-y-5">
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Provincial nomination is not a unilateral decision. Each year, Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada (IRCC) sets a national PNP target as part of the multi-year Immigration Levels Plan. That national figure is then divided among the participating provinces and territories based on a federal-provincial allocation formula that considers population, labour-market needs, and historic uptake. British Columbia receives one of the larger shares, but the absolute number has dropped sharply since 2024.</p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">For 2024, BC PNP had approximately 8,000 nominations. For 2025, the initial allocation was halved to roughly 4,000 (later topped up to 6,214). The 2026 allocation started at 5,254 and was raised to 6,254 on August 18, 2026. This reduction does not reflect declining demand for skilled workers in BC - the province continues to face documented labour shortages in healthcare, tech, construction, and education. Rather, the cut reflects federal policy decisions about overall immigration volumes following the post-pandemic surge and capacity constraints in housing and public services.</p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Within the 2026 envelope, BC chooses how to distribute invitations across its streams. Since April 23, 2026 it has done so through targeted Care and Build rounds and Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds, with no tech-only or general points-only draws. Your occupation and credentials decide which contest you are in, and your score decides your rank within it. This is the most important contextual fact for any applicant currently in or considering the registration pool.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Sector Case Studies -->
            <section>
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Sector Impact in 2026</h2>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Healthcare</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">BC's six health authorities continue to issue direct invitations to internationally educated physicians, registered nurses, and nurse practitioners. There is no SIRS minimum for these direct-offer roles and the path to PR remains the fastest in Canada. The Health Authority direct stream is expected to consume up to one-third of all 2026 nominations.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Tech</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">There were no tech-only draws in 2026; the final priority tech draw was December 3, 2024. Tech workers compete in Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds, which used points cutoffs of 131 to 138 and wage thresholds that fell from $70/hr to $49/hr during 2026. See our guide for <a href="/articles/tech-workers-bc-pnp-2027-after-tech-draws-ended" class="text-green-600 underline">tech workers after the end of tech draws</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Childcare and Education</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Early Childhood Educators (NOC 42202) get targeted Care: Childcare rounds. From May to October 2026 these issued 719 invitations, with cutoffs easing from 115 to 99. Applicants need a BC Early Childhood Educator certificate and a job offer from an eligible BC employer.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Construction Trades</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The first Build: Construction Trades round took place on May 6, 2026, and Build rounds issued 791 invitations from May to October, with cutoffs between 88 and 108. Candidates need SkilledTradesBC certification or a registered apprenticeship matching their job. The federal Express Entry trades category also ran rounds in 2026, giving applicants two complementary pathways.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Hospitality and Tourism</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Entry-level and semi-skilled tourism roles became significantly harder to nominate in 2026 due to allocation cuts. Existing workers in Whistler, Victoria, and Tofino should consider switching to the Skilled Worker stream via TEER 3 supervisory roles for better odds.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Pro Tips -->
            <section class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Pro Tips for 2026 Applicants</h2>
                <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                    <li><strong>Watch BC PNP draw history monthly.</strong> The province publishes invitation cut-offs after each draw. Patterns reveal which streams are favoured each quarter.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-green-600 underline">BC PNP calculator</a> often.</strong> Score requirements move; recalculate every 90 days, especially after changes to wage, role, or language.</li>
                    <li><strong>Consider Atlantic Canada or Manitoba as a backup.</strong> Other provinces face similar allocation pressure but have different eligibility profiles. Diversifying your provincial strategy is wise in 2026.</li>
                    <li><strong>Keep a federal Express Entry profile active at all times.</strong> A high CRS (470+) can produce an ITA without PNP involvement, especially under category-based draws for healthcare and STEM occupations.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

