          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-6">Reading the 2026 Allocation Signals</h2>
            <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The 2026 federal allocation to British Columbia under the Provincial Nominee Program started at 5,254 nominations and is now 6,254 after the August 18 increase, covering both Express Entry BC and non-Express Entry nominations. That is below the 2024 level of 8,000 and about the same as 2025's final 6,214. BC PNP uses it through three objectives: Care (health care, childcare, education and veterinary care), Build (9 construction trades) and Innovate (High Economic Impact). There are no tech-only draws.</p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Applicants in administrative, sales, marketing, finance, and hospitality NOCs that are still eligible compete in Innovate, where points cutoffs ran from 131 to 138 in 2026. By contrast, Care: Health rounds reached 76 points in September and October 2026, Childcare 99, and Build 94. The lowest published cutoff of the year was 50, in the July 23 Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative round.</p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">Use the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-red-600 underline">BC PNP score calculator</a> to model where you fall, then read the section below to understand whether you should reposition into a priority NOC or aim for the Innovate cutoff.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

