        <section class="mt-8">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-6">Pro Tips: Repositioning for Priority NOCs</h2>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
              <li><strong>Map adjacent NOCs.</strong> A marketing manager (NOC 10022) may pivot to a Computer and Information Systems Manager (NOC 20012) by changing employer or role within the same company.</li>
              <li><strong>Use the ECE shortcut.</strong> ECE Assistants (NOC 42202) require only a one-year certificate from a BC institution. International students with any bachelor's degree can complete the bridging certificate at Capilano, VCC, or Douglas in 10-12 months.</li>
              <li><strong>Construction trades certification.</strong> Red Seal certification in carpentry, plumbing, or electrical opens NOC 72xxx access. ITA BC offers a Trades Training Assessment that often credits foreign experience.</li>
              <li><strong>Healthcare bridging programs.</strong> Internationally Educated Nurses can complete the BCCNM substantive equivalent assessment in 8-14 weeks and join the Health Authority stream.</li>
              <li><strong>Regional pivots.</strong> Moving a tech or healthcare role from Vancouver to Prince George, Cranbrook, or Fort St. John earns 15 area points, plus 10 more for regional experience or regional alumni status where you qualify.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-8">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 mb-6">Allocation Priority FAQ</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              <details class="group bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
                <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-5 cursor-pointer list-none">Will general draws disappear entirely in 2026?</summary>
                <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">In practice, yes. Since the April 23, 2026 priorities, the BC PNP has run targeted Care and Build rounds and Innovate (High Economic Impact) rounds rather than general points-only draws. The Innovate points track is the closest equivalent, with cutoffs of 131 to 138 in 2026, compared with the 100-110 seen in 2023.</div>
              </details>
              <details class="group bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
                <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-5 cursor-pointer list-none">Are the priority sector quotas published officially?</summary>
                <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. BC PNP does not publish hard sub-quotas. The figures above are our sums of published invitations, not quotas.</div>
              </details>
              <details class="group bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
                <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-5 cursor-pointer list-none">Does the federal allocation cut apply to Express Entry-aligned BC PNP streams?</summary>
                <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Yes. BC's 2026 allocation (5,254, raised to 6,254 on August 18, 2026) covers both base and Express Entry-enhanced provincial nominations from BC.</div>
              </details>
              <details class="group bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
                <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-slate-900 p-5 cursor-pointer list-none">If I am invited late in 2026, can my application roll into 2027 quota?</summary>
                <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Nominations issued before December 31 are counted against the 2026 allocation regardless of when IRCC issues COPR. BC PNP typically pauses December draws to ensure the year-end count is accurate.</div>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

